host-neutral engine (decided, VD 2026-09-08; docs/decisions.md dec-B85)
  after the merge to main, before the 1.0-0 CRAN submission (VD
  2026-09-23: an interface change consumers build against, so it lands
  before the header becomes the 1.0 contract): internal plain-C spec
  stage in the bridge; the engine's Rmath touchpoints through
  src/external's ext_* names only (model.hpp calls Rf_pnorm5, Rf_dnorm4
  and Rf_dt directly today), the implementation chosen at build time -
  Rmath under R (bit-identical), a vendored copy of R's nmath pnorm,
  qnorm, dnorm and dt otherwise (VD 2026-09-24: no run-time reason for
  hooks, no SIMD variant and no BLAS use); qchisq stays host-side, the
  spec carrying the resolved residual-prior scale; printing likewise
  build-time (dec-A74): ext_printf, ext_fflush_stdout and the message
  and warning entries map per host (R console, stdio for a standalone
  build), and the unused misc_printf/misc_flushOutput pointers and
  their installers go; a plain-spec creation entry and a host-neutral
  error contract in dbarts.h covering EVERY entry point, not creation
  alone (VD 2026-09-27, dec-B119): a refusal returns a status the host
  reads instead of raising an R error, the unwind to the entry point
  staying as it is; and a plain-C getter for each chain's current sigma,
  riding the same header change so consumers rebuild once (VD
  2026-09-27, dec-A73). The sister re-verification at the freeze
  follows it. Rewords the Python-binding entry.
  Predictor source consolidation (decided, VD 2026-09-24; dec-B124),
  same arc: the spec's predictor part is dbarts_predictor_source, so the
  R parse and the flat entries fill one struct and pass one
  translate-and-validate stage (translateSource, today flat-only) into
  bartcore::PredictorSource. The header takes the engine's addressing:
  dbarts_predictor_source gains denseChannels (per column, >= 0 double
  column, < 0 code column ~k), denseCodes is read only through it, and
  the kind-selected single columnSources index goes. Retires the
  per-bridge bounds and channel sweeps and the int32 -> ColumnKind /
  xint_t copies; columnTypes and categoryCounts gain a reader. Refusals
  that name a column by its R name stay in the R bridge. Owed: the
  in-tree C consumer test, an ABI hash re-bake, a stan4bart rebuild
  (dense helper only, no source change).

frequency weights (decided, VD 2026-09-24; docs/decisions.md dec-B125)
  after the merge to main, before the 1.0-0 CRAN submission: an explicit
  opt-in to frequency weights (a row stands for w identical
  observations) beside the precision default, on the families where
  that is a coherent model. Gaussian is certain: sigma's posterior df
  nu + sum(w) over the weighted SSR, the sigest df sum(w) - p, the ppd
  and log-likelihood at sd sigma with each row counted w times; leaf
  updates unchanged. A design note first settles the family list
  (Student-t, heteroscedastic, AFT, probit and ordinal carry a per-row
  latent or scale, so w copies are not one row reweighted; logistic's
  counts are already binomial trials, a different thing), the
  argument's name and shape on bart, dbarts, the sampler and dbarts.h,
  whether non-integer frequency weights are refused, and zero weights
  (left out of the likelihood and kept in the design, as now). The weights docs say which a weight is. The note
  also answers the maintainer's question of 2026-10-05, what the
  package pays for one weights argument meaning a precision (gaussian,
  Student-t), a trial count (logistic) and membership (0/1 on probit and
  ordinal, installed as the row mask): whether probit and ordinal keep
  taking 0/1 weights as a mask or take membership by its own name, so
  that each of the three has one spelling. Measured on a loaded machine,
  20000 rows, 50 trees: the weighted path costs about 14 percent over
  the unweighted one, an all-ones mask nothing, and a row masked out
  costs as much per sweep as an active row (half the rows masked 1.14
  of the full unmasked time, the active half alone 0.55).

engine constants audit (decided, VD 2026-09-08; docs/decisions.md
  dec-B91; LANDED; plan docs/plans/engine-performance.md)
  post-release: a size gate on the fused roll + suffstat pass, two
  thresholds rather than one - fuse weighted at n >= 1e4, unweighted at
  n >= 2e4 - since the fusion loses up to 8 percent below those and
  wins up to 24 above, and one threshold cannot serve both families
  (at n = 1e4 it would have to fuse weighted and decline unweighted);
  leaf occupancy is a worse key than n and is not even monotone in the
  gain. It must arrive WITH equivalence scenarios sized above the
  threshold: every fit in all three corpora today is under 2000 rows,
  so a gate would revert all 75 to the stock association and leave the
  fused pass with no equivalence coverage at all. Crossover grid and
  corpus count: docs/plans/engine-performance.md, "Crossover grid for
  a size gate".

