binary-hyperprior-revisit (raised by the study, 2026-09-14;
  docs/plans/binary-hyperprior.md, "What to revisit after the mixing
  work"; post-release, gated on the mixing line of inquiry)
  the sampled k does not converge at any length that study could pay
  for: 64,000 draws per fit reach an effective sample size of 29 and a
  split-Rhat that never falls below 1.2. Three of its results rest on
  that - every sampled-k number in the note is a chain-length summary
  rather than a posterior one, the residual 0.008 of coverage
  shortfall still showing at the probe could be the sampler or the
  model, and the interior optimum is located by coverage-distance
  differences of 0.002 to 0.005, the same order as the 0.012 the
  incumbent's own coverage still moved between the long length and the
  probe, so the optimum's location is not resolved to better than
  about a factor of two in scale. To reopen the default, a mixing fix
  has to show two things: the sampled k at a split-Rhat below 1.05
  with an effective sample size in the hundreds at a length a user
  would actually run, and then, at that length, either a spread in
  coverage distance across the finite-scale arms larger than 0.02 or
  an optimum somewhere other than scale 0.5 to 2. If the spread stays
  at half a point of coverage, no mixing fix reopens the question; it
  only makes the verdict better founded. Re-run with
  benchmarks/R/binary-hyperprior.R. The fixed-k results, the largest
  effects in the note, involve no sampled k and do not depend on this.

